  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global Integral Ecology Peacebuilding Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Peacebuilding is a key component of the Middle East, Sri Lanka, Myanmar, Andes and South Sudan strategies and will remain a focus of core programmes. Building on programming experience and on synergies which exist between the country programmes, the proposed initiative will apply an integral ecology lens to peacebuilding and explore the role of the environment in peacebuilding. The overall aim of the project is to facilitate innovation in peacebuilding programming at CAFOD. To do this, the project will create spaces for partners and project participants to implement pilot projects, to share experiences and will encourage a culture of encounter which can lead to an increased sense of hope and solidarity for young people in situations of protracted crisis as well as an openness to new ideas. The project will seek to draw out learning and disseminate this through stories of successes and the exchange of methodologies which have proven to have impact. The project will include:

1) Pilot initiatives: partners will implement innovative pilot projects which enable an exploration of how applying an Integral Ecology lens can contribute to more effective Peacebuilding programmes. 

2) Peer to peer learning:
a) Between CAFOD partners: through virtual exchanges and sharing of methodologies, case studies and success stories.
b)Between project participants: Participants will exchange stories of successes and challenges and of the ways in which they have been able to contribute to positive changes  in their communities. 
c)Between CAFOD staff: The project will create opportunities for colleagues across CAFOD to collaborate and learn from proven approaches in order to promote and strengthen peacebuilding work.

2) Learning: development of case studies, toolkits and learning synthesis highlighting successful approaches and methodologies to different elements of peacebuilding programming.

3) Engage with supporters in E&amp;W and to conduct effective global advocacy on peacebuilding.</narrative>
  </description>
